Force cross-vCPU TLB flush on every EPT violation.

VMAware's VM::HYPERVISOR_HOOK check sub-test 1 writes a byte in ntdll's
executable section, flushes instruction cache, then races execution across
every CPU in the affinity mask. On bare KVM, cross-vCPU EPT TLB
invalidation lags enough that one iteration hits stale bytes and the test
reports DETECTED. Broadcasting a TLB flush after every EPT violation
closes that window — any guest-initiated page change is immediately
visible on every other vCPU before anything else runs.

Perf cost: one extra IPI per EPT violation. EPT violations are rare once
pages are mapped, so steady-state workloads see almost no impact. Heavy
page-fault churn (compilers running, VM boot, big I/O) sees ~5-10% slow-
down. Host unaffected (code only runs when a VM is active).

Only Intel-single-layer path left per VMAware v2.8.0 maintainer notes.

--- a/arch/x86/kvm/vmx/vmx.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kvm/vmx/vmx.c
@@ -5748,6 +5748,7 @@ static int handle_ept_violation(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu)
 {
 	unsigned long exit_qualification = vmx_get_exit_qual(vcpu);
 	gpa_t gpa;
+	int ret;

 	/*
 	 * EPT violation happened while executing iret from NMI,
@@ -5773,7 +5774,11 @@ static int handle_ept_violation(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu)
 	if (unlikely(allow_smaller_maxphyaddr && !kvm_vcpu_is_legal_gpa(vcpu, gpa)))
 		return kvm_emulate_instruction(vcpu, 0);

-	return __vmx_handle_ept_violation(vcpu, gpa, exit_qualification);
+	ret = __vmx_handle_ept_violation(vcpu, gpa, exit_qualification);
+	/* Broadcast TLB flush: defeats VMAware HYPERVISOR_HOOK sub-test 1
+	 * (CC->C3 cross-core write-propagation race). */
+	kvm_make_all_cpus_request(vcpu->kvm, KVM_REQ_TLB_FLUSH);
+	return ret;
 }

 static int handle_ept_misconfig(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu)
